        </div><div class="article-template__content page-width page-width--narrow rte scroll-trigger animate--slide-in">
          <div class="rocket-shopify-article lngvty-article" style="max-width:880px;margin:0 auto;color:#181818;font-family:'Avenir Next',Avenir,'Century Gothic','Trebuchet MS',sans-serif;font-size:17px;line-height:1.75;letter-spacing:.15px;">

    
    <p style="max-width:760px;margin:0 0 24px;color:rgba(24,24,24,.68);font-size:20px;line-height:1.5;letter-spacing:.2px;">
        A practical routine for oily but dehydrated, acne-prone skin balances gentle cleansing, water-binding hydration, and targeted pore clearing without stripping the skin barrier.
    </p>

    
    <div style="margin:0 0 38px;padding:20px 22px;border-left:4px solid #181818;background:#FFFEF3;">
        <p style="margin:0;color:#181818;">
            <strong style="font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.5px;">Quick answer:</strong>
            Manage oily but dehydrated, acne-prone skin with a gentle cleanser, a barrier-conscious active, and an oil-free gel moisturiser. The main trade-off is clearing pore congestion without depleting surface water, because harsh drying washes damage the barrier and trigger rebound oil. Start by paring your routine down to a mild cleanser, a single calming active serum, a ceramide lotion, and a daily fluid sunscreen.
        </p>
    </div>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        If your forehead and nose are shiny by midday while your cheeks feel tight, your skin is probably oily and dehydrated at the same time. People often assume oily skin has plenty of moisture, but oil and water are two different things. Sebum is the surface oil your oil glands produce, while hydration is the water held inside your skin cells. Skin can make plenty of oil and still be parched for water.
    </p>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        When dehydrated skin breaks out, scrubbing it with harsh foaming washes, drying toners, and strong spot treatments almost always makes things worse. Stripping the surface weakens your moisture barrier, making pores more reactive and easier to clog. To clear breakouts and calm excess shine, you have to protect your barrier and add water back into your skin instead of drying it out.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        Why skin can be oily, dehydrated, and acne-prone at once
    </h2>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Oil and water live in different places in your skin. Oil glands make sebum to protect against friction. Genetics, hormones, and climate decide how much oil you make. Hydration is different: it is the water held inside outer skin cells, sealed by fats like ceramides and cholesterol.
    </p>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        When that barrier is damaged, water escapes into the air quickly. Research shows people with acne often have a weaker barrier and higher water loss than people with clear skin.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39502708/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color:#B87330;text-decoration:none;">1</a></sup> Lacking water, dead cells cannot shed smoothly. They stick together, mix with oil, and plug pore openings, forming blackheads and inflamed spots.
    </p>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Daily conditions in India make this worse. Moving between humid heat and air-conditioned rooms pulls moisture from your face while oil accumulates. Hard tap water leaves deposits that disrupt the surface, and harsh scrubbing parches the barrier while oil glands keep producing.
    </p>

    <ul style="margin:0 0 22px;padding-left:22px;">
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Midday shine with morning tightness.</strong> Your face feels tight right after washing, but turns shiny within two hours.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Flaking around breakouts.</strong> Dry flakes cling to red blemishes or your nose, making sunscreen look patchy.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Stinging from gentle products.</strong> Even plain, fragrance-free lotions or sunscreens tingle or sting on contact.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Rough texture under surface oil.</strong> Your skin looks shiny, but feels rough and bumpy when touched.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        The core mistake: treating oil while ignoring dehydration
    </h2>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        The most common mistake is treating oily, breakout-prone skin like dirty glass that needs degreasing. When a cleanser leaves your face squeaky, it has washed away the ceramides and moisture-binding proteins needed to hold water inside. Dermatologists warn that aggressive cleansing breaks down the barrier and makes acne treatments harder to tolerate.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/24765221/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color:#B87330;text-decoration:none;">2</a></sup> Clinical trials confirm that combining a mild cleanser with a barrier-supporting moisturiser improves acne and calms irritation far better than washing aggressively.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/25483138/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color:#B87330;text-decoration:none;">3</a></sup>
    </p>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Stacking several drying treatments creates the same problem. Using an acid wash, a strong toner, a spot treatment, and a retinoid together overwhelms the skin, flaring redness and delaying healing.
    </p>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        A calmer approach works better: pick one active serum that clears pores without stripping moisture. The <a href="/products/acne-blemish-control-serum-30ml" style="color:#181818;text-decoration-color:#B87330;text-decoration-thickness:2px;text-underline-offset:4px;">Acne, Blemish Control Serum</a> clears pore congestion with 1% salicylic acid while five ceramides, cholesterol, and Centella asiatica support the barrier and soothe visible redness.
    </p>

    
    <div style="margin:42px 0;padding:30px 26px;background:#181818;color:#FFFFFF;">
