    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        The step-by-step daily routine for oily, dehydrated skin
    </h2>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        A good routine has two goals: hydrate and shield in the morning, and clear congestion while repairing your barrier at night.
    </p>

    <h3 style="margin:28px 0 14px;color:#181818;font-size:22px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;">
        Morning routine: hydrate and shield
    </h3>
    <ol style="margin:0 0 22px;padding-left:24px;">
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Cleanse gently with lukewarm water.</strong> Use a low-pH, non-stripping gel wash. If your cheeks feel tight, a splash of lukewarm water rinses away sweat without stripping surface fats.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Hydrate on damp skin.</strong> Pat a light hydrating toner or watery essence with glycerin or panthenol onto damp skin so humectants bind water effectively.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Seal with an oil-free, ceramide gel moisturiser.</strong> Skipping moisturiser lets water evaporate, prompting more oil. An oil-free gel with ceramides restores barrier lipids without clogging pores.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37605504/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color:#B87330;text-decoration:none;">4</a></sup>
</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Protect with fluid broad-spectrum sunscreen.</strong> Apply non-comedogenic SPF 50 sunscreen. Sun exposure worsens inflammation and turns acne marks into dark spots. A fluid matte sunscreen shields skin without greasiness.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/34812859/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color:#B87330;text-decoration:none;">5</a></sup>
</li>
    </ol>

    <h3 style="margin:28px 0 14px;color:#181818;font-size:22px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;">
        Evening routine: clear and rebuild
    </h3>
    <ol style="margin:0 0 22px;padding-left:24px;">
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Wash away sunscreen and grime.</strong> Cleanse with your gentle gel wash. If wearing water-resistant sunscreen, use micellar water first, followed by your water-based cleanser.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Apply your active serum.</strong> Smooth 3 to 4 drops of <a href="/products/acne-blemish-control-serum-30ml" style="color:#181818;text-decoration-color:#B87330;text-decoration-thickness:2px;text-underline-offset:4px;">Acne, Blemish Control Serum</a> over damp skin across the acne-prone area. Its 1% salicylic acid unclogs pores, niacinamide regulates daytime sebum, and Centella asiatica calms redness.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16766489/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color:#B87330;text-decoration:none;">6</a>,<a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40682377/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color:#B87330;text-decoration:none;">7</a></sup> This is your only active; do not layer extra acids.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Lock in recovery with your moisturiser.</strong> Follow with your ceramide moisturiser to prevent overnight water loss and ensure active acids remain comfortable without peeling.<sup><a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39835475/" rel="noopener noreferrer" target="_blank" style="color:#B87330;text-decoration:none;">8</a></sup>
</li>
    </ol>

    <div style="margin:34px 0;padding:24px 26px;border-top:1px solid #B87330;border-bottom:1px solid #B87330;background:#FFFEF3;">
        <p style="margin:0 0 7px;color:#B87330;font-size:12px;line-height:1.2;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;">Routine rule</p>
        <p style="margin:0;color:#181818;">
            Never apply exfoliating acids to stinging skin. If basic moisturiser stings, pause all active serums for three to five days and stick to a gentle wash, a ceramide lotion, and sunscreen until your barrier recovers.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        Ingredients to choose and ingredients to avoid
    </h2>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        An effective routine picks ingredients that clear pores without degrading the barrier fats that keep water in the skin:
    </p>

    <div style="margin:24px 0 34px;overflow-x:auto;border-top:2px solid #181818;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.25);">
        <table style="width:100%;min-width:620px;border-collapse:collapse;text-align:left;font-size:15px;line-height:1.55;">
            <thead>
                <tr style="background:#FFFEF3;">
                    <th style="padding:15px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.25);font-size:12px;letter-spacing:1.2px;text-transform:uppercase;">Ingredient</th>
                    <th style="padding:15px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.25);font-size:12px;letter-spacing:1.2px;text-transform:uppercase;">Primary role</th>
                    <th style="padding:15px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.25);font-size:12px;letter-spacing:1.2px;text-transform:uppercase;">What to expect</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.14);font-weight:700;">Salicylic acid (1% BHA)</td>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.14);">Dissolves oil and dead cells inside pores</td>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.14);">Fewer blackheads and blemishes over 2 to 4 weeks</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.14);font-weight:700;">Niacinamide</td>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.14);">Regulates sebum and supports ceramides</td>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.14);">Less shine and calmer post-blemish redness</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.14);font-weight:700;">Ceramides and cholesterol</td>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.14);">Rebuild the moisture barrier matrix</td>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.14);">Less tightness, flaking, and stinging after washing</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;font-weight:700;">Centella asiatica (cica)</td>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;">Soothes inflammation and irritation</td>
                    <td style="padding:16px 14px;">Skin tolerates daily pore-clearing actives more easily</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Equally important is avoiding products that weaken your barrier or clog pores:
    </p>

    <ul style="margin:0 0 22px;padding-left:22px;">
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>High-alcohol astringents.</strong> Toners loaded with denatured alcohol strip barrier fats and cause rebound oiliness.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Rough physical scrubs.</strong> Walnut shells and stiff brushes tear delicate skin, worsening redness.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Heavy occlusive balms.</strong> Pure petrolatum and thick plant butters trap heat and sebum inside follicles.</li>
        <li style="margin:0 0 10px;">
<strong>Alkaline bar soaps.</strong> Traditional soaps disrupt the acidic barrier and wash away essential lipids.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 style="margin:52px 0 16px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        Realistic timelines and what to expect
    </h2>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Restoring balance takes patience because you are clearing pores while repairing your moisture barrier.
    </p>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        In the first one to two weeks, the immediate gain is comfort. Tightness after washing eases, basic lotions stop stinging, and skin looks less flaky around blemishes.
    </p>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        Between weeks three and four, pore clearance and oil balance become visible. In the serum's own clinical study on 36 Indian women, ages 18 to 30, with acne, lesion count was down 26.2% at two weeks and 47.7% at four weeks, with comedones down 61.6% and sebum down 19.4% in four weeks. Results may vary.
    </p>
    <p style="margin:0 0 22px;">
        By weeks six to eight, old blemish marks fade and shine stays under control. If you experience deep cystic nodules or severe scarring, consult a qualified dermatologist.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin:56px 0 18px;color:#181818;font-size:34px;line-height:1.12;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-1.15px;">
        Frequently asked questions
    </h2>
    <div style="border-top:2px solid #181818;">
        <div style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Can skin be oily and dehydrated at the same time?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">Yes. Sebum from oil glands and water in skin cells are separate systems. A damaged barrier lets water escape while oil glands keep producing sebum.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Why does skin produce more oil when dehydrated?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">When your skin barrier lacks water, it becomes irritated. Oil glands often produce extra sebum to compensate for the missing surface moisture, creating tightness and shine together.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Should I skip moisturiser if my face gets oily?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">No. Skipping moisturiser accelerates water loss, prompting oil glands to produce more oil. Use a light, oil-free gel with ceramides to seal in water without clogging pores.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">How often should I apply an acne serum if my skin reacts easily?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">Patch test first and start every other night. Once your skin feels comfortable without peeling after two weeks, move to once daily in the evening.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">Can I use salicylic acid and niacinamide together?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">Yes. Salicylic acid clears debris from inside pores while niacinamide regulates sebum and supports the barrier. Using them in one balanced serum avoids the irritation of layering multiple products.</p>
        </div>
        <div style="padding:22px 0;border-bottom:1px solid rgba(24,24,24,.2);">
            <h3 style="margin:0 0 8px;color:#181818;font-size:21px;line-height:1.3;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:-.25px;">What should I do if moisturiser stings when applied?</h3>
            <p style="margin:0;">Stinging means your skin barrier is damaged. Pause all exfoliating acids and scrubs immediately. Use only a mild wash, a ceramide lotion, and sunscreen for three to five days until stinging stops.</p>
        </div>
    </div>

